      "Language": "spa",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "In patients with hypercholesterolaemia determinate the prevalence of high coronary risk (CR), study the lipid lowering treatment applied and determinate if there is any change in CR after a period of treatment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Cross-sectional."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "EMPLACEMENT",
          "content": "Primary care."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "583 patients with hypercholesterolaemia both sex, older than 25 years registered in chronic mobility, randomized selected."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENT AND RESULTS",
          "content": "Applying the Framingham coronary multivariate risk method we estimate high CR > 20%. Patients with a previous history of cardiovascular event, were treated in a 50%, more frequently younger subjects, rising 220 mg/dl of final cholesterol level. Patients without any cardiovascular event known, the 32.5% (28.0-36.7%) have a CR > 20%. Subjects with high CR have 4.9 (3.0-8.2) more probability if receiving treatment than the others with lower risk. The lipid-lowering treatment is explained in a 67% because the high CR and the family history of coronary event. After at least one year period there is a reduction in those with high CR (difference relative of proportions 28.7% [20.4-37.1])."
        }
      ]},
